// CPR/First Aid Practice Test 1. Original questions written for Valus, modeled on the
// HOSA 2026–27 Round One test plan (Basic Life Support 40%, First Aid 60%) and sample items.
// Content follows the AHA 2025 guidelines and ARC 2025 guidelines the event now uses.
const AHA_BLS='AHA Basic Life Support', AHA_FA='AHA Heartsaver First Aid', ARC_BLS='ARC Basic Life Support', ARC_FA='ARC First Aid/CPR/AED', DHO='Simmers, DHO: Health Science';
const q=(topic,prompt,options,answer,explain,ref)=>({topic,prompt,options,answer,explain,ref});

export default {
 id:'cpr-first-aid-1',
 event:'CPR/First Aid',
 title:'Practice Test 1',
 subtitle:'Full-length Round One simulation',
 minutes:60,
 plan:[
  {area:'Basic Life Support',weight:40,topics:['General Concepts','BLS/CPR for Adults, Children and Infants','AED for Adults, Children and Infants','CPR with Advanced Airway','Mouth-to-Mouth/Rescue Breathing','Relief of Choking']},
  {area:'First Aid',weight:60,topics:['Basic','Medical Emergencies','Injury Emergencies','Environmental Emergencies']}
 ],
 questions:[
  q('General Concepts','A rescuer finds an adult lying on the floor who does not respond when tapped and shouted at. The person takes a slow, irregular gasp every 10 seconds. How should the rescuer interpret this breathing?',['As normal breathing; place the person in the recovery position','As a sign of cardiac arrest; begin CPR if no pulse is felt','As an airway obstruction; begin abdominal thrusts','As a seizure; protect the head and wait for it to end'],1,'Agonal gasps are not normal breathing. An unresponsive person who is only gasping is treated as being in cardiac arrest: activate EMS, get an AED and start CPR when no definite pulse is felt.',AHA_BLS),
  q('Basic','A first aider arrives at a car crash and sees an injured driver lying in the roadway. What should the first aider do first?',['Begin a head-to-toe check of the driver','Drag the driver to the side of the road','Check the scene for traffic, fire and other hazards','Ask bystanders what happened before the crash'],2,'Every emergency begins by checking the scene for safety. A rescuer who rushes into traffic can become a second victim. Once the scene is safe, the rescuer checks the person, calls 911 and gives care.',ARC_FA),
  q('Environmental Emergencies','A football player has been practicing in high heat. Which finding most clearly suggests heat stroke rather than heat exhaustion?',['Confusion and slurred speech','Heavy sweating and pale, clammy skin','Thirst and painful muscle cramps','Headache, nausea and dizziness'],0,'A change in mental status (confusion, slurred speech, seizures, unresponsiveness) is the key sign that separates heat stroke from heat exhaustion. Heat stroke is life-threatening and needs 911 and rapid cooling.',AHA_FA),
  q('BLS/CPR for Adults, Children and Infants','What is the correct compression depth for high-quality CPR on an adult?',['1 to 1.5 inches (2.5 to 4 cm)','About 1 inch (2.5 cm)','3 to 3.5 inches (7.5 to 9 cm)','2 to 2.4 inches (5 to 6 cm)'],3,'Adult compressions should be at least 2 inches (5 cm) deep but not more than 2.4 inches (6 cm), at a rate of 100–120 per minute, with full chest recoil after each compression.',AHA_BLS),
  q('Injury Emergencies','A worker has a deep cut on the thigh. Bright red blood keeps spurting even though firm direct pressure is being held. A commercial tourniquet is available. Where should it be placed?',['Directly over the wound','2–3 inches above the wound, not over a joint','Just below the wound, between the wound and the knee','Over the knee joint so it cannot slip'],1,'For life-threatening limb bleeding that direct pressure does not control, place a tourniquet 2–3 inches above the wound (closer to the heart), avoiding joints. Tighten until bleeding stops and note the time it was applied.',ARC_FA),
  q('Medical Emergencies','A 52-year-old man has crushing chest pressure that spreads to his left arm, along with sweating and nausea. 911 has been called. He has no allergy to aspirin and no bleeding problems. What should the first aider do next?',['Have him walk around to see if the pain eases','Give him a friend’s nitroglycerin tablet','Have him lie flat with legs raised and give him water','Have him rest and help him chew aspirin'],3,'These are signs of a heart attack. After calling 911, keep the person at rest and, if there is no allergy, bleeding problem or instruction against it, have them chew one adult aspirin or two to four low-dose aspirin. Never give someone else’s prescription medicine.',AHA_FA),
  q('AED for Adults, Children and Infants','An AED arrives while CPR is being performed on an adult. What is the first step in using the AED?',['Turn the AED on','Attach the pads to the bare chest','Press the shock button','Stop CPR for a full pulse check'],0,'The first step is always to power on the AED. Its voice prompts then guide the rescuer through attaching the pads, analyzing the rhythm and delivering a shock if one is advised.',AHA_BLS),
  q('Basic','A woman has collapsed in a mall and does not respond to tapping or shouting. Why is it legally acceptable to give her first aid without asking permission?',['Expressed consent was given by a bystander','Good Samaritan laws require all bystanders to act','Implied consent applies to people who cannot respond','Consent is never needed for first aid'],2,'Implied consent assumes that a person who is unresponsive, or otherwise unable to respond, would want help. A responsive adult must give expressed consent before care begins.',ARC_FA),
  q('Relief of Choking','A responsive adult at a restaurant is clutching his throat and cannot speak or cough. Under the current AHA and Red Cross guidelines, what should the rescuer do?',['Give abdominal thrusts only until the object comes out','Alternate 5 back blows and 5 abdominal thrusts','Perform a blind finger sweep, then give back blows','Encourage him to drink water to wash the object down'],1,'The current AHA and Red Cross guidelines teach the same method for a responsive adult or child with severe choking: alternate 5 back blows between the shoulder blades and 5 abdominal thrusts until the object comes out or the person becomes unresponsive.',AHA_BLS+'; '+ARC_FA),
  q('Injury Emergencies','A teenager spills boiling water on her forearm, causing redness and pain. What is the most appropriate first step after making sure the scene is safe?',['Run cool water over it for at least 10 minutes','Hold an ice pack directly on the burn for 20 minutes','Spread butter or burn ointment over the area','Pop any blisters so the heat can escape'],0,'Cool a thermal burn with cool (not ice-cold) running water for at least 10 minutes. Ice damages tissue, and butter or ointment traps heat and raises the risk of infection. Blisters should be left intact.',AHA_FA),
  q('Medical Emergencies','An older man suddenly has a drooping face, weakness in his right arm and slurred speech. After calling 911, which piece of information is most important to give EMS?',['His blood pressure reading right now','What he ate and drank today','When his symptoms started','The names of all his medications'],2,'Stroke treatments such as clot-dissolving medicine depend on how much time has passed. The T in BE FAST means Time: note when symptoms began or when the person was last known to be well and tell EMS.',AHA_FA),
  q('General Concepts','During BLS assessment of an unresponsive adult, how long should the rescuer take to check for breathing and a pulse at the same time?',['No more than 2 seconds','At least 15 but no more than 20 seconds','At least 30 seconds','At least 5 but no more than 10 seconds'],3,'Check breathing and the carotid pulse together for at least 5 but no more than 10 seconds. If no definite pulse is felt in 10 seconds, begin CPR starting with compressions.',AHA_BLS),
  q('Environmental Emergencies','A runner at a summer race is confused, has a very high body temperature and hot skin. EMS has been called. What is the most effective way to cool her?',['Give her a sports drink and have her rest in the shade','Immerse her body up to the neck in cool or cold water','Cover her with a dry blanket and fan her face only','Place a single ice pack on her forehead'],1,'Heat stroke needs the fastest possible cooling. Cold-water immersion up to the neck works best. If immersion is not possible, sponge with cool water, fan, and place ice packs at the neck, armpits and groin. A confused person should not drink.',AHA_FA),
  q('BLS/CPR for Adults, Children and Infants','Two healthcare providers are performing CPR on a 6-year-old child. What compression-to-ventilation ratio should they use?',['30:2','5:1','15:2','10:1'],2,'For children and infants, one rescuer uses 30:2. When two rescuers are present, the ratio changes to 15:2 to give more frequent breaths.',AHA_BLS),
  q('Injury Emergencies','A first aider is holding pressure on a bleeding scalp wound, and blood soaks through the gauze. What should the first aider do?',['Remove the soaked gauze and start over with clean gauze','Lift the gauze every minute to check whether bleeding has stopped','Rinse the wound with water to see the source','Place more dressings on top and continue firm pressure'],3,'Removing a soaked dressing can pull away clots that are forming. Leave it in place, add more dressings on top and keep applying firm, steady pressure.',ARC_FA),
  q('Medical Emergencies','A classmate with diabetes is shaky, sweaty and confused, but she is awake and able to swallow. What should the first aider give her?',['15–20 grams of fast-acting sugar','A dose of her own insulin by injection','Nothing by mouth until EMS arrives','A large meal rich in protein and fat'],0,'Low blood sugar can be treated with 15–20 grams of fast-acting sugar when the person can sit up and swallow. Recheck in about 15 minutes, and call 911 if she does not improve or cannot swallow safely.',AHA_FA),
  q('CPR with Advanced Airway','Two rescuers are performing CPR on an adult who now has an advanced airway in place. How should compressions and breaths be given?',['30 compressions, then a pause for 2 breaths','Nonstop compressions, plus 1 breath every 6 seconds','15 compressions, then a pause for 2 breaths','Nonstop compressions, plus 1 breath every 2 seconds'],1,'Once an advanced airway is in place, rescuers stop pausing for breaths. One rescuer gives continuous compressions at 100–120 per minute while the other gives 1 breath every 6 seconds (10 breaths per minute).',AHA_BLS),
  q('Basic','A 9-year-old boy has fallen off his bike and has a scraped, bleeding knee. His mother is standing next to him. Who must give consent before first aid is given?',['The mother, as his parent or guardian','The boy, because he is responsive','No one, because it is a minor injury','A police officer or other authority figure'],0,'Minors cannot legally give consent. When a parent or guardian is present, ask that adult for consent. If no parent or guardian is present and the injury is serious, consent is implied.',ARC_FA),
  q('AED for Adults, Children and Infants','A 5-year-old is in cardiac arrest. The AED that arrives has only adult pads. What should the rescuers do?',['Skip the AED and continue CPR only','Cut the adult pads in half to fit the chest','Use the adult pads, placed so they do not touch','Wait for EMS to bring pediatric pads'],2,'Pediatric pads or a dose attenuator are preferred for children under 8. If they are not available, use adult pads. On a small chest, place one pad on the front and one on the back so they do not touch.',AHA_BLS),
  q('Injury Emergencies','A lab worker has a dry chemical powder on his arm that is burning his skin. What should the first aider do first after putting on gloves?',['Flush the powder with water right away','Brush off the powder, then flush with water','Cover the area with a dry, sterile dressing','Apply a neutralizing chemical to the skin'],1,'Brush dry chemicals off first, because some react with water. Then flush with large amounts of cool running water for at least 20 minutes while removing contaminated clothing and jewelry. Do not try to neutralize the chemical.',ARC_FA),
  q('BLS/CPR for Adults, Children and Infants','A lone rescuer is performing CPR on an infant. According to the current guidelines, which compression technique should be used?',['Two fingers placed in the center of the chest','Both hands, one on top of the other, like adult CPR','Three fingers placed on the tip of the breastbone','Heel of one hand, or two thumbs encircling the chest'],3,'The updated guidelines no longer teach the two-finger technique. A lone rescuer uses the heel of one hand or two thumbs with the hands encircling the chest, on the lower half of the breastbone just below the nipple line.',AHA_BLS+'; '+ARC_BLS),
  q('Medical Emergencies','A teenager with a known seizure disorder is having a seizure in the school hallway. Which situation means 911 should be called?',['It is her first seizure today','She is drowsy for a few minutes afterward','The seizure lasts longer than 5 minutes','Her friends know she has epilepsy'],2,'Call 911 if a seizure lasts more than 5 minutes, another seizure follows, the person is injured, pregnant, has diabetes, is in water, or does not wake up. During any seizure, protect the head, move hard objects away and never restrain the person or put anything in their mouth.',AHA_FA),
  q('Mouth-to-Mouth/Rescue Breathing','An unresponsive adult is not breathing normally but has a definite carotid pulse. How should the rescuer give rescue breaths?',['1 breath every 6 seconds; pulse check about every 2 minutes','1 breath every 2–3 seconds; pulse check every 30 seconds','2 breaths every 30 seconds; pulse check every 5 minutes','1 breath every 10 seconds; no further pulse checks'],0,'An adult with a pulse who is not breathing normally gets 1 breath every 6 seconds (about 10 breaths per minute), each over 1 second with visible chest rise. Recheck the pulse about every 2 minutes and start CPR if it is lost.',AHA_BLS),
  q('Environmental Emergencies','A hiker is found shivering, with slurred speech and cold, wet clothing. Which action is correct?',['Rub her arms and legs briskly to restore circulation','Give her hot coffee or alcohol to warm her from inside','Place her directly into a hot bath to warm her fast','Move her somewhere warm, remove wet clothes, rewarm slowly'],3,'For hypothermia, move the person out of the cold, handle them gently, remove wet clothing and warm the body gradually with dry blankets. Rubbing, hot baths, caffeine and alcohol can cause harm.',ARC_FA),
  q('Basic','When should a first aider move an injured person before giving care?',['Whenever the person would be more comfortable somewhere else','Only when there is immediate danger or it is needed to give care','Always, so the person can be placed flat on their back','Only after a 911 dispatcher gives permission by phone'],1,'Moving an injured person can make injuries worse, especially spinal injuries. Move a person only when there is immediate danger, such as fire, traffic or rising water, or when they must be moved to reach another victim or give lifesaving care.',ARC_FA),
  q('General Concepts','Which rescuer action reduces the quality of CPR?',['Pushing at a rate of 100–120 compressions per minute','Keeping interruptions in compressions under 10 seconds','Leaning on the chest between compressions','Switching compressors about every 2 minutes'],2,'Leaning on the chest prevents full chest recoil, so the heart cannot fill with blood between compressions. High-quality CPR allows full recoil after every compression.',AHA_BLS),
  q('Injury Emergencies','A worker was shocked by a power line that has now been turned off by the utility company. He has small burns on his hand and foot. What is the greatest immediate danger?',['Cardiac arrest or an abnormal heart rhythm','A skin infection developing at the burn sites','Dehydration from fluid lost through the burns','A broken bone in the hand from the fall'],0,'Electricity passing through the body can stop the heart or cause a dangerous rhythm, and internal damage is often worse than the small entry and exit burns suggest. Be ready to give CPR and use an AED.',ARC_FA),
  q('Medical Emergencies','A student stung by a bee has hives, a swollen tongue and trouble breathing. She has a prescribed epinephrine auto-injector. Where should it be injected?',['The upper arm, near the shoulder','The outer middle of the thigh','The abdomen, near the navel','Directly into the sting site'],1,'An epinephrine auto-injector is given in the outer middle of the thigh, and it can go through clothing. Hold it in place for the time the device instructions say, then call 911 and note the time it was given.',AHA_FA),
  q('BLS/CPR for Adults, Children and Infants','How deep should chest compressions be for an infant?',['About 1.5 inches (4 cm)','About 0.5 inch (1 cm)','About 2 inches (5 cm)','About 2.5 inches (6 cm)'],0,'Infant compressions should be at least one-third the depth of the chest, about 1.5 inches (4 cm). For children, the depth is at least one-third the chest depth, about 2 inches (5 cm).',AHA_BLS),
  q('Injury Emergencies','A first aider is splinting a suspected broken forearm. Which statement about the splint is correct?',['It should cover only the injured spot on the forearm','The bone should be straightened before the splint goes on','It should immobilize the joints above and below the injury','Circulation only needs to be checked before splinting'],2,'Splint the injury in the position found and immobilize the joints above and below it (the wrist and elbow for a forearm). Check circulation, sensation and movement both before and after splinting.',DHO),
  q('Basic','Which statement best describes Good Samaritan laws?',['They require all trained bystanders to give care at every emergency','They protect only healthcare professionals while they are on duty','They allow rescuers to charge a reasonable fee for emergency care','They protect those who give reasonable care in good faith'],3,'Good Samaritan laws protect people who willingly give emergency care in good faith, within the limits of their training, without expecting payment, and without gross negligence.',DHO),
  q('Relief of Choking','A choking adult becomes unresponsive while a rescuer is giving back blows and abdominal thrusts. After lowering the person to the ground and calling EMS, what should the rescuer do?',['Begin CPR, checking the mouth before giving breaths','Roll the person face down and give 5 more back blows','Do a blind finger sweep, then begin CPR','Wait for EMS to arrive with suction equipment'],0,'Begin CPR starting with compressions. Each time the airway is opened to give breaths, look in the mouth and remove an object only if you can see it. Blind finger sweeps can push the object deeper.',AHA_BLS),
  q('Environmental Emergencies','A snowboarder has frostbitten fingers and still has a long walk to the lodge. Which action is correct?',['Rub the fingers with snow to restore feeling','Hold the fingers near a campfire to warm them quickly','Do not rewarm the fingers if there is a chance they could refreeze','Break any blisters so the tissue can drain'],2,'Thawing and then refreezing causes more tissue damage, so do not rewarm frostbite if it may freeze again. Never rub the area or use direct dry heat, and leave blisters intact.',AHA_FA),
  q('Medical Emergencies','A first aider gave naloxone nasal spray to an unresponsive person with a suspected opioid overdose. After 2–3 minutes, the person still is not breathing normally. What should be done?',['Wait at least 30 minutes before doing anything more','Leave the person alone to go look for more naloxone','Spray the rest of the device into the person’s mouth','Give a second dose in the other nostril'],3,'If there is no response within 2–3 minutes, give another dose of naloxone in the other nostril. Keep supporting breathing or giving CPR, and stay with the person, because naloxone can wear off before the opioid does.',AHA_FA),
  q('AED for Adults, Children and Infants','A rescuer preparing to apply AED pads notices a hard lump under the skin of the patient’s upper right chest, likely an implanted pacemaker. What should the rescuer do?',['Skip the AED because the shock could damage the device','Place the pad at least 1 inch away from the device','Place the pad directly over the device','Use only one pad, on the left side of the chest'],1,'Do not delay defibrillation. Avoid placing a pad directly over an implanted device: keep the pad at least 1 inch away from it, or use front-and-back placement.',AHA_BLS),
  q('Injury Emergencies','A construction worker has a metal rod stuck in his thigh. What should the first aider do?',['Pull the rod out quickly to reduce pain','Remove the rod, then apply direct pressure','Push the rod further in to stop the bleeding','Leave the rod in place and stabilize it with bulky dressings'],3,'Removing an impaled object can cause severe bleeding. Leave it in place, control bleeding around it and use bulky dressings to keep it from moving.',ARC_FA),
  q('BLS/CPR for Adults, Children and Infants','Where should a healthcare provider check the pulse of an unresponsive infant?',['Carotid artery, side of the neck','Radial artery, thumb side of the wrist','Brachial artery, inside of the upper arm','Dorsalis pedis artery, top of the foot'],2,'Check the brachial pulse in infants, on the inside of the upper arm between the elbow and shoulder. For adults and children, use the carotid pulse (the femoral pulse may also be used in children).',AHA_BLS),
  q('Basic','A first aider has finished bandaging a wound and taken off their gloves. What should they do next?',['Put the gloves back in the first aid kit for later use','Wash their hands with soap and water for at least 20 seconds','Rinse their hands only if blood is visible on them','Apply lotion to prevent skin irritation'],1,'Always wash hands with soap and water after removing gloves, even when the gloves looked clean. Use an alcohol-based hand sanitizer if soap and water are not available. Used gloves go in a biohazard bag or the trash and are never reused.',DHO),
  q('General Concepts','Two rescuers are performing adult CPR. How often should they switch the compressor role to prevent fatigue?',['About every 2 minutes (5 cycles of 30:2)','Every 30 seconds','Every 10 minutes','Only when the compressor says they are tired'],0,'Compressor fatigue lowers CPR quality within minutes, often before the rescuer notices. Switch about every 2 minutes, or 5 cycles of 30:2, and make the switch in less than 5 seconds.',AHA_BLS),
  q('Medical Emergencies','A student with asthma is wheezing and struggling to breathe. She has her prescribed quick-relief inhaler. How should the first aider help?',['Have her lie flat on her back','Help her sit upright and use her inhaler','Give her a glass of water to calm the airway','Have her breathe into a paper bag'],1,'Help the person sit upright, which makes breathing easier, and help them use their own prescribed inhaler. Call 911 if breathing does not improve or the person cannot speak in full sentences.',AHA_FA),
  q('Injury Emergencies','A man fell from a ladder. He is responsive, has neck pain and tingling in his hands, and the scene is safe. What should the first aider do?',['Keep his head and neck still in the position found and call 911','Help him sit up so he can breathe more easily','Have him turn his head side to side to check movement','Place a pillow under his head and roll him onto his side'],0,'Suspect a spinal injury after a fall with neck pain or tingling. Keep the person still and hold the head and neck in the position found, unless the person must be moved to give CPR or escape danger. Call 911.',ARC_FA),
  q('Environmental Emergencies','A youth soccer game is being played when thunder is heard in the distance. What should the coach do?',['Keep playing until lightning is actually seen','Have players shelter under the tallest nearby tree','Have players lie flat in the middle of the field','Move everyone indoors or into hard-topped vehicles'],3,'When thunder roars, go indoors. Lightning can strike miles from a storm, and open fields and tall trees are dangerous. Wait at least 30 minutes after the last thunder before going back outside.',ARC_FA),
  q('CPR with Advanced Airway','Why should rescuers avoid giving too many or too-forceful breaths during CPR with an advanced airway?',['It wears out the bag-mask device and its valve quickly','It makes the advanced airway tube more likely to slip out','It raises chest pressure and reduces blood return to the heart','It causes the patient to regain consciousness too quickly'],2,'Excessive ventilation raises pressure inside the chest, which reduces blood return to the heart and lowers the blood flow that CPR produces. Give each breath over about 1 second with just enough volume to make the chest rise.',AHA_BLS),
  q('Basic','A first aider uses the SAMPLE history while talking with an ill person. What does the L stand for?',['Level of consciousness','Location of pain','Length of illness','Last oral intake (last food or drink)'],3,'SAMPLE stands for Signs and symptoms, Allergies, Medications, Pertinent past medical history, Last oral intake (last food or drink), and Events leading up to the emergency.',ARC_FA),
  q('AED for Adults, Children and Infants','An AED has just delivered a shock to an adult in cardiac arrest. What should the rescuers do immediately?',['Check for a pulse for 10 seconds','Give 2 rescue breaths and then check the rhythm','Remove the pads and wait for EMS','Resume CPR, starting with chest compressions'],3,'Resume CPR right away, starting with compressions, after a shock. The AED reanalyzes the rhythm about every 2 minutes. Pausing to check for a pulse delays the compressions the heart needs.',AHA_BLS),
  q('Medical Emergencies','A toddler is found with an open bottle of household cleaner and may have swallowed some. The child is awake and breathing normally. What should be done?',['Call Poison Help and follow their directions','Make the child vomit as soon as possible','Give the child milk and watch for symptoms','Do nothing unless symptoms begin to develop'],0,'For a responsive person who may have swallowed a poison, call Poison Help at 1-800-222-1222 and follow their directions. Do not make the person vomit unless told to. Call 911 if the person has trouble breathing, seizures or becomes unresponsive.',AHA_FA),
  q('Injury Emergencies','After a serious bleeding injury, a patient is restless, with pale, cool, clammy skin and a rapid, weak pulse. What should the first aider do after calling 911?',['Give food and water to raise blood sugar','Have the patient walk to improve circulation','Have the patient lie down and keep them warm','Place cold packs on the chest to slow the heart'],2,'These are signs of shock. Have the person lie flat, control any bleeding, keep them warm with a blanket and give nothing to eat or drink. Monitor breathing until EMS arrives.',ARC_FA),
  q('BLS/CPR for Adults, Children and Infants','A lone rescuer with no phone finds an unresponsive 4-year-old who was not seen collapsing. The child is not breathing and has no pulse. What should the rescuer do next?',['Leave right away to call 911, then return to start CPR','Give about 2 minutes of CPR, then go call 911 and get an AED','Give 2 breaths, then wait to see if the child starts breathing','Give rescue breaths only until someone else arrives to help'],1,'Children often go into cardiac arrest because of a breathing problem. If the collapse was not witnessed and you are alone with no phone, give about 2 minutes of CPR first, then leave to call 911 and get an AED. For a sudden collapse that someone saw, call and get the AED first.',AHA_BLS),
  q('Mouth-to-Mouth/Rescue Breathing','A 3-year-old is unresponsive and not breathing normally but has a pulse of 90 beats per minute. At what rate should rescue breaths be given?',['1 breath every 6 seconds','2 breaths every 15 seconds','1 breath every 10 seconds','1 breath every 2–3 seconds'],3,'Children and infants who have a pulse but are not breathing normally get 1 breath every 2–3 seconds (20–30 breaths per minute). If the pulse falls below 60 per minute with signs of poor perfusion, start CPR.',AHA_BLS),
  q('Environmental Emergencies','A camper is bitten on the ankle by a snake that may be venomous. Which first aid action is correct?',['Apply a tight tourniquet above the bite','Keep the person still and remove tight items','Cut over the bite and suck out the venom','Pack the bite in ice to slow the venom'],1,'Keep the person still and calm, remove rings, shoes and other tight items before swelling starts, and get emergency care. Tourniquets, cutting, suction and ice are not recommended for snakebites.',AHA_FA)
 ]
};
